---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="About Umi-Time">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <main class="about">
      <section class="hero">
        <h1>About Umi‑Time</h1>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time expresses the 24‑hour UTC day as a continuous 360‑umi cycle.
          Every location on Earth shares the same umi value at the same moment,
          providing a universal representation of time that remains directly
          compatible with UTC.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>What Is Umi‑Time?</h2>

        <p>
          Umi‑Time is a layered representation of UTC. The universal Umi‑Time
          value is the same everywhere on Earth, while local interpretation is
          derived from the observer's longitude and solar context may be added
          separately.
        </p>

        <p>
          This separation allows a single shared time value to remain globally
          consistent while still providing meaningful local context. Universal
          time, local interpretation, and solar context are treated as distinct
          layers rather than being combined into a single regional clock
          reading.
        </p>

        <p>
          Umi‑Time builds directly on UTC, preserving its stability and
          precision while presenting the day in a form that is easier to share,
          compare, and reason about across regions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Why Umi‑Time Exists</h2>
        <p>
          Civil time was built for regions, borders, and policies — not for a
          world connected by global networks, distributed teams, online
          communities, and continuous international communication. It fragments
          the day into zones, offsets, and seasonal rules that differ from place
          to place and change over time.
        </p>
        <p>
          UTC solved many of these problems by providing a single, stable
          reference for global coordination. Umi‑Time builds on that foundation,
          offering a clear, continuous representation of the UTC day that is
          intuitive for people and straightforward for systems.
        </p>
        <p>
          Developed by Daniel Brannon in 2026, Umi‑Time is designed to make our
          clocks universal and local at the same time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>What Makes Umi‑Time Different</h2>
        <p>Umi‑Time is:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Universal:</strong> one shared value for the entire world, derived
            directly from UTC.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Layered:</strong> universal time, local interpretation, and solar
            context are treated as separate concerns.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Locally meaningful:</strong> longitude provides local interpretation
            without changing the underlying time value.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Human‑friendly:</strong> the 360‑umi cycle is easy to visualize,
            compare, and communicate.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Compatible:</strong> integrates cleanly with UTC and systems that
            already rely on it.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time provides a shared planetary reference for time while
          preserving the local and solar context needed to make that reference
          meaningful to people.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
